   Papo was standing in the middle of the aisle with his mouth slack,
murmuring, “Ocho. Ocho. Ocho.”
   “Siete, bruto!” said Wilson, and slapped him in the back of the head.
   Papo came up out of it like a man surfacing. “What did you call
me?”
   “Bruto. It’s seven. It was always seven.”
   “It’s eight!”
   The block was waking up.
   It wasn’t the people who’d been hurt the most, or the people who
knew the most. It was Doña Fela, in her hat, looking at the attendant
who had put a hand on her elbow to move her, and saying in a voice
that cut through a hundred and ten hertz like a knife through flan: “No
me toques.”
   The attendant let go.
   She sat down.
   She sat down in the middle of the aisle on the white carpet, in her
hat, with her purse on her knees, folded her hands, and said, loud
enough for the whole hall to hear: “No me muevo.”
   I will not be moved.
   Somebody laughed. Then Don Chino sat down beside her, and his
wife, and Teodoro, still holding his poem, and Papo, because Wilson
sat down, and Wilson, because Papo did. In four seconds there was a
row of senior citizens sitting across the main aisle of the Kingsbridge
Armory like a sandbag wall, with Doña Fela at the center, her hat at a
very fine angle.
   The attendants stopped.
   They stood there with their earbuds in. They’d been trained for a
participant who ran. They had a protocol for a participant who
shouted. Nobody had told them what to do about eight grandparents
and a poem.
   “Sir,” said one of them, to Don Chino, with real distress, “sir, you
can’t block the aisle.”



